<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Docref on mixmix</title><link>https://mixmix.nz/experiment/docref/</link><description>Recent content in Docref on mixmix</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 15:00:00 +1300</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://mixmix.nz/experiment/docref/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Loose Ends</title><link>https://mixmix.nz/experiment/docref/2026_11_16/</link><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 15:00:00 +1300</pubDate><guid>https://mixmix.nz/experiment/docref/2026_11_16/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://mixmix.nz/experiment/docref/2026_11_16/loose-ends.jpg" alt="Featured image of post Loose Ends" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;In January Estonia passed a gambling tax amendment that left out one word and accidentally made online
casinos tax-free for a year. Luukas Ilves (their former government CIO) gave the amendment and the Act
it changed to a couple of LLMs, and both spotted the gap straight away. Within a day he&amp;rsquo;d built
&lt;em&gt;Apsakaleidja&lt;/em&gt; (&amp;ldquo;mistake finder&amp;rdquo;), which checks every bill going through their parliament.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Docref seemed like a good place to try that for New Zealand. Every provision has a stable address, so
&amp;ldquo;section 73 of the Electricity Industry Act 2010&amp;rdquo; can be resolved to an actual block of text and checked.&lt;/p&gt;
