&lt;p&gt;I asked Claude to run five simple detectors over the 70 Acts and regulations on docref. They look for
references to repealed or non-existent sections, mentions of Acts the corpus says are repealed, borrowed
definitions that point at the wrong section, and drift between versions. The detectors flagged 433
suspects. Claude then read each one and sorted it into real error, cured by the Legislation Act&amp;rsquo;s
&amp;ldquo;references to repealed legislation&amp;rdquo; rule, deliberate transitional wording, or noise. 13 held up, ranked
by confidence. Each one can be walked through from the citing provision to what it actually points at,
with links to the pinpoint on docref.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My favourites:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the Gas Act still gives powers to an &amp;ldquo;Energy Commission&amp;rdquo; that lives in a subpart that isn&amp;rsquo;t there&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;the Regulatory Standards Act 2025 overrides a Public Service Act section that was repealed about six
months after it passed&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Most of these are loose ends rather than true contradictions, but they&amp;rsquo;re objective, and someone who
knows the area can check each one in a few minutes. That seems like the right first test of whether this
approach finds real things. The next step would be getting an expert to check them, then aiming the same
pairwise review at harder cases where two live provisions actually conflict.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Contentious Acts</title><link>https://mixmix.nz/experiment/docref/2026_11_15-b/</link><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 09:00:00 +1300</pubDate><guid>https://mixmix.nz/experiment/docref/2026_11_15-b/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://mixmix.nz/experiment/docref/2026_11_15-b/contention.jpg" alt="Featured image of post Contentious Acts" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;The overview of changes was interesting, but words changed isn&amp;rsquo;t a reliable signal
of impact of those changes. Thinking about ways to measure or reveal that in the dataset
I got to wondering whether we could detect contention around an act - perhaps the signal
would be changes and reversions by successive governments, or intense editing of the
same stretches of legislation.&lt;/p&gt;
